WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.680
Some video games have compelling characters,
great stories, and epic plot twists.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.680
Others…

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.080
don't, leaving us with more disappointment
and questions than answers.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.650
Sure, every great game may have a few, small
storytelling inconsistencies, but others are

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:14.650
just plain inexcusable.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.040
Here are a few the worst of the worst.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.650
And be warned, spoilers ahead.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.000
Final Fantasy VII

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.660
It's safe to assume Final Fantasy VII is most
fans' favorite game in the series.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.279
But one question has nagged even casual players
for ages: why didn't someone just use a Phoenix

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:32.689
Down on Aeris after she's killed by Sephiroth?

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.110
It's an item that literally brings someone
back to life, and they're used hundreds of

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:37.280
times throughout the series…

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.890
so why not Aeris?

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.110
Why not now?

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.739
Some might argue that Phoenix Downs can only
revive characters who are just NEAR death

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:45.460
— but let's really think about this.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.980
Your heroes get pounded by boulders, burned
with fire magic, sliced with fridge-sized

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.000
swords, and eradicated by giant saw-boned
eels.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.800
Is getting stabbed by Sephiroth's sword somehow
worse than all that?

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.800
Apparently.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.270
But no one knows why.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.270
Heavy Rain

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:04.629
Quantic Dreams' cinematic interactive drama
Heavy Rain is a unique experience, with a

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:06.939
great story and a twist nobody saw coming.

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:12.130
That said, it's also full of plot holes, including
one very major one we can't simply ignore.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:16.499
Early in the game, we learn that central protagonist
Ethan Mars has blackouts following the death

00:01:16.499 --> 00:01:17.700
of one of his sons.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.939
He wakes up in seemingly random locations,
soaking wet, holding origami figures.

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:25.499
That sounds like pretty bad news when there's
an "Origami Killer" on the loose.

00:01:25.499 --> 00:01:29.000
Does Ethan has some kind of split personality
serial killer disorder?

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.640
The problem is, after we learn what's really
going on, and who the Killer really is, Ethan's

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.049
blackouts and handfuls of origami are never
explained.

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:40.329
They just occur to distract and confuse the
player, and we never get to find out why,

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:45.020
making them seem like a pretty lame and obvious
attempt at throwing us off the trail.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.020
Metroid

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:50.789
A classic Nintendo franchise, Metroid's premise
is awesome: you're a lady in a badass robotic

00:01:50.789 --> 00:01:52.429
suit that kills aliens.

00:01:52.429 --> 00:01:53.429
What's not to like?

00:01:53.429 --> 00:01:54.700
There is one problem, though.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.189
For a game series that prides itself on continuity
and realism, we've killed the dinosaur space

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:01.649
pirate Ridley a few too many times.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:05.849
Even the most adept Metroid scholars have
a hard time explaining the constant reappearances

00:02:05.849 --> 00:02:09.149
of Ridley, one of the series' most notorious
bosses.

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:12.530
Those who've played every Metroid game may
be forgiven for thinking that they've killed

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:17.870
Ridley almost a dozen times in Metroid, Super
Metroid, Metroid Prime, Metroid Prime 3, and

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:18.870
Metroid Fusion.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.849
Apparently, the guy just didn't die in all
of those cases, and has been cloned multiple

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:23.849
times.

00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:25.159
At least that's how Nintendo makes sense of
it all.

00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:28.129
But maybe it's time to let poor Ridley rest
in peace.

00:02:28.129 --> 00:02:29.650
Fallout 3

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:34.049
There's nothing quite like a massive plot
hole after a 60-plus hour trek through post-apocalyptic

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:35.109
nuclear wastelands.

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:39.640
At the end of Fallout 3, you're presented
with a choice: either you or your companion

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.909
have to enter an irradiated chamber.

00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:45.840
But either way, somebody's going to get a
lethal dose of X-rays, and it's not going

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:46.840
to be pretty.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.609
Those players rolling with the super mutant
companion Fawkes, however, were left wondering

00:02:50.609 --> 00:02:52.590
why we couldn't send him into the chamber.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.180
You know, considering he's immune to radiation.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:59.260
It would basically be a leisurely stroll for
Fawkes, so why won't he just do what any normal

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.530
mutant would do?

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.239
Short answer: the game's developer, Bethesda,
decided to make Fawkes a super mutant jerk.

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:10.430
"Uhhhhh, I'm sorry my companion but, uh, no."

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:14.110
He refuses to enter the chamber, going on
about how he doesn't want to rob us of our

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:18.719
destiny and offering other bogus reasons,
none of which excuse the fact that he's immune

00:03:18.719 --> 00:03:19.749
to radiation.

00:03:19.749 --> 00:03:24.020
Bethesda eventually fixed the weird choice
with the Broken Steel add-on, but for fans,

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:28.110
the damage was done, and we were sent off
with a big ol' Fawkes you.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.340
Red Dead Redemption

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.109
The open-world action-adventure Red Dead Redemption
is a master-class in game making, and easily

00:03:34.109 --> 00:03:38.709
the best Western-themed video game ever made,
but it also illustrates the fact that no game

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:39.709
is perfect.

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:43.200
And there's one significant plot hole which
really made players scratch their ten-gallon

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.720
hats in bewilderment.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.230
Everyone was bummed out when John Marston
died at the end of the main story.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:52.690
Luckily, his son Jack shows up to save the
day three years later, doling out some revenge

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.280
and being an all-around badass — just like
his father.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.329
The problem is, this turn of events poses
one major question: how in tarnation did book-readin'

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:05.950
intellectual Jack Marston turn into a gunslinger
on par with his dad in only three years?

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.530
Marston Senior surely cultivated that precision
aim and those legendary skills after a lifetime

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:14.920
of being an outlaw, in addition to all the
shenanigans we actually have control over.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:20.049
We have to assume that Jack, presumably still
in his late teens, acquires every one of his

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:24.020
father's talents, without his father to teach
him, in just three short years.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.110
We're calling bullchips on this one.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:27.630
Crysis 2

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:31.919
In the first Crysis, we learn the hard way
that a race of invading aliens can actually

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:36.050
absorb nuclear energy — like the kind that
comes from nuclear weapons — which makes

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:37.550
them more powerful than ever.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:38.650
Who knew, right?

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:43.310
So when it came time for the sequel, one might've
assume there'd be no nuking of aliens.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:47.960
Defying all logic and common sense, the military
in Crysis 2 orders a nuclear strike on New

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:52.449
York City in an attempt at eradicating the
same alien threat, basically pretending the

00:04:52.449 --> 00:04:53.990
first game never happened.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:58.370
It's not even a minor oversight; the latter
portion of the second game is based entirely

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.710
around nuking New York City with alien-fortifying
bombs.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.830
You're basically dropping vitamins on them.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.919
You'd think a failed nuking of an energy-absorbing
alien race wouldn't be something the military

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:12.040
would forget — but apparently in Crysis
2, that file got lost somewhere.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.290
Thanks for watching!

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.400
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.500
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

